it's always fun to go back in time power  of the Daleks now this will be the first  of what will hopefully become a semi  regular fixture on this channel which is  my reviewing classic era Doctor Who  stories now I have seen a few in my time  I've still got a lot of others to get  through it and as I see them I do plan  to do reviews on them but I need to do  something a little bit especially  because power of the Daleks premiered  very recently now this is an older story  in fact the very first second doctor  story but it has been lost I mean the  video footage does not exist anymore but  all the audio survived so between that  some still images and script notes and  what they know the thing was supposed to  look like BBC has put together an  animated version using all the original  audio and that was presented ahead of  this past Christmases Doctor Who  Christmas specials so power the Daleks  what do I think it's pretty good  I mean it's dated in a way that a lot of  older doctor who is IIe you've got  things like you we only have maybe a  dozen characters but they try and tell  you there's this whole colony of people  so you kind of feel the constraints of  the budget is one of the six part  stories so you can clip that they  stretched it a little thinner than it  probably should have been I think a four  part sure would be fine and in what  feels a little bit like a holdover from  the tail end of the first doctors tenure  the doctor himself kind of gets taken  out of commission a little bit he gets  jaded at one point there were lengthy  stretches where we don't get a whole ton  of him and I think part of that is also  due to the fact that they kind of over  complicated the story going on I in the  location where he and his companions  have ended up and then trying to explain  all that and untangle the web of  motivations of everybody who's already  there kind of just necessitates  sidelining the doctor a little but as  far as what the plot is this is as I  said the first story following the  regeneration of the first doctor into  the second and he and his two companions  Ben and Polly who are companions I'm not  particularly familiar with but they land  on this planet Vulcan in a  they never specified a time period but  it presumes some sort of future you know  relative to where we are and Vulcan is a  colony with some sort of twisty internal  politics going on and in the middle of  this a scientist has discovered a  capsule and inside are three Daleks that  appear to be dead but you know dialects  and so scientists fascinated by this  discovery is trying to get them  reactivated while the doctor is  desperately trying to get anyone around  so listen to him do explain that these  things must be destroyed now one of the  first things that I caught that was  really interesting was realizing how  much has been intentionally borrowed  from well this episode and classic  Doctor Who in general in new Doctor Who  I mean very specifically the dog that's  going I am your servant which got reused  in victory of the Daleks so I  immediately recognized that and made me  appreciate victory of the Daleks a  little bit more that they brought that  back and recycled it caches sometimes  when they do that like the whole Daleks  are the masters of Earth was it who that  was used in the stolen earth that was a  callback to some classic Doctor Who so I  like when I learn more stuff that and  that has been repurposed in and been  called back to in the new series and  that's always fun for me to discover it  was really interesting to see how kind  of fully formed the notion of  regeneration seemed to be right out of  the gate and by that I just find it  really interesting that they seem to  know well I'll have to be very vague and  loose about it in terms of explaining  exactly what happened in exactly why and  how the doctor is different we can very  clearly see but at points the doctor  refers to meet past doctors the doctor  doesn't even really refer to himself at  all you're like what is going on and  he's figuring out you know his new south  and he gets hold of the little pipe his  ridiculous hat at one point but he's off  by just a little bit  he knows he's off by a little bit and  just all the wonkiness that we have come  to expect from regeneration it's just  fascinating for me to find out now by  getting to see the fact that was all  there right from the beginning there's  stuff that I don't know if I had  actually intellectually thought about it  but it was  assumed must have been added in later  and refined as the show went on but to  realize how much stuff this series  nailed right out of the gate back in the  60s it's it's just wonderful as I said  though the story is kind of a product of  its time it but if you like classic era  Doctor Who you'll probably find  something to enjoy in this it's hard to  recommend as as any kind of early look  into classic Doctor Who for people who  are more up-to-date on new who but  haven't gotten back into the classic era  so far and part of the reason for that  is I think doing it animated hurts it a  little bit now having said that I'm  don't I don't regret that they did an  animator and I would much rather see it  animated in this fashion than not see it  at all but it's very very limited  animation it's very obvious that they  didn't spend a whole ton of money on  this animation and you get used to it in  terms of you know if you watch any sort  of chunky animation for life you just  kind of get you suing you roll with it  but where it falls down is that there  are there will be shots and there will  be moments that the nothing really  happens and you know that in the  live-action you would have that would  have been the actors chance to do  something you know with their face and  your really get a read on you know  what's going on you know what are they  thinking hmm but with these really rigid  barely moving facial expressions that  are really kind of locked in they just  kind of they haven't looked back and  forth and that's about it and you really  you feel those pauses a lot more than  you're supposed to because obviously  with real actors they would have been  able to communicate more in their  physical performance that you know they  didn't they didn't have the budget to  him to do that level of animation and  that level of nuance and so that that  kind of hurts a little bit versus if we  actually still had the real footage  again though if you are sort of used to  the pacing of classic Doctor Who and if  you enjoy that at all definitely check  this out if you missed it so those are  my thoughts on power of the Daleks did  you see it what did you think about it  whatever you thought drop something down  in the comments and let's talk about it  don't forget to like subscribe hit that  little  down there so you get notifications when  new stuff goes up follow me on Twitter  at Council of geeks check out the  council of geeks podcast available on  iTunes and stitcher I put links down the  scriptures for those and until next time  this council is adjourned
 

